 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Activation Function Mismatch:** The uniform assumption may not hold for different activation functions in INRs. If $\epsilon_i$ does not follow a uniform distribution, the calculated variance will be incorrect [S1].
-*   **Intermediate Layer Bounds:** Distribution estimation only happens at the final INR output. Intermediate layers still use "hard" bounds to calculate $\gamma$ (the threshold for iso-surface extraction). This introduces intervals containing low-probability values, leading to pessimistic results, especially in deep networks [S1].
-*   **CLT Assumptions:** The independence, Lindeberg’s condition, and large number assumptions cannot be strictly proved for INR weights, as no restrictions are placed on them. However, heuristics suggest they hold in practice [S1].
